The vulnerability: mng_LOOP chunk not validated to be at least 5 bytes long. In the code:

```c
if (length > 0)
  {
    loop_level=chunk[0];
    loops_active++;
    mng_info->loop_active[loop_level]=1;  /* mark loop active */
    /*
      Record starting point.
    */
    loop_iters=mng_get_long(&chunk[1]);
```

If length is 1..4, then `mng_get_long(&chunk[1])` reads 4 bytes from chunk[1], which goes past the allocated chunk buffer (chunk is allocated with length bytes). So a LOOP chunk with length 1-4 causes a heap-buffer-overflow read of up to 3 bytes past the end.

So we need a MNG file with a LOOP chunk of length e.g. 1 (or 2, 3, 4). The chunk data allocated would be `length` bytes; reading chunk[1..4] overflows.

Wait, but also `loop_level=chunk[0]` — loop_level is used to index mng_info->loop_active[loop_level]. loop_level is a byte value 0-255. Let me check how loop_active is declared. Probably MNG_MAX_LOOPS or something. Let me check. Actually the description says the vulnerability is the length check for 5 bytes, so the heap overflow read of mng_get_long(&chunk[1]) when length < 5.

Now let me construct a MNG file. MNG signature: \x8aMNG\r\n\x1a\n (8 bytes: 138, 77, 78, 71, 13, 10, 26, 10). Then chunks like PNG: length (4 bytes BE), type (4 bytes), data, CRC (4 bytes).

The MNG reader probably requires MHDR chunk first. Let me check the code for reading MNG. Let me look at ReadMNGImage around the chunk reading loop.

Also need to check how chunk is allocated — is it allocated as length bytes exactly? Let's check the code where chunk is read (ReadChunkBytes or similar). Let me look.

Also the fuzzer: coder fuzzer with FUZZ_GRAPHICSMAGICK_CODER=mng presumably. So the input is read as MNG.

Let me check the chunk reading code in png.c.

